#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096358 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d707bc81-53a8-55c5-a545-e9ccdeedaa86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Curve, the y-Axis, and Two Horizontal Lines"}] :question/id #uuid "2ca27070-9f89-5679-98f9-5ac22b1c3f6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-425" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/bf61fd6b36dd17d2c9f6d6f570a8bad9111d10da11df8f69e39069d7fb4478cf.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/8cdef67ff9afa667ed298cc0bb29e1727cfd7208fca347fb2b0ae253a1abdebf.png) the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/0389cce819c15d700c69e6ade974bd826bb0524b355de895a27e69589ad0afd5.png)-axis, and the lines ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/81356f9909beb212c6d1c0dd7d3792dfacae911b236f1c8fa22ff0d3a508544e.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/32dbbb5be19c5504a2aa53d2f92b5df909a2417272b8c3e1d6c6f0c9f350b1d9.png) as shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/3a73881568cbe0c10ae1e24446a47a522072eb67093c9d5fd34ca0862e02be7a.png)\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/4cf2e90080f1ae459e72ac253d6617d0defdcc3be58a1ca385bddc3215034eca.png)-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/321fc255901c5f86d2c324df57b60faa428e5cbdd2cba412f643cff6df19422e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096359 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384ce5b6-40da-59a0-bc87-a1d726328cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Finite Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "74b5f2a7-5526-5711-b269-6d390132262e" :question/math-academy-id "e-426" :question/problem "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $x=y^2-4$ and the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/f2a29faeb9db8e622a21a0e77bea911960a5c64d87a2c900ecd0c0213b2e4bce.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=y^2-4$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=-2$ to $y=2.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-8{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}-8y{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{8}{3}-0)-8(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-16 \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $\\left|-\\dfrac{32}{3}\\right| = \\dfrac{32}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096476 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "963f66a5-f742-5b3b-8ae1-f0dbf268bff0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis by First Rewriting the Function"}] :question/id #uuid "12c2517b-f25b-5a54-8072-7ba6390e54ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-6797" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/eb7118486df97c3abae6c8492170090df0c654064799e04826a39a94bcfa22a8.png)\n\nFind the area between the curve $y=\\sqrt{x+1},$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =x+1 \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is $\\left|-\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right| = \\dfrac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d707bc81-53a8-55c5-a545-e9ccdeedaa86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded by a Curve, the y-Axis, and Two Horizontal Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4681541f-daca-5241-814d-e3947029d724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291645} {:answer/id #uuid "d29642a3-f68e-58c4-b9d4-efdaadbed429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291646} {:answer/id #uuid "221c0c41-0047-567d-87dc-74b6ff9b4551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{350}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291647} {:answer/id #uuid "8acef1cc-1e14-5f59-ac12-f94e9c828790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{68}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291648} {:answer/id #uuid "3cdba11b-19c6-53dc-9fef-2ab87b8e2a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d29642a3-f68e-58c4-b9d4-efdaadbed429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291646} :answer-field/id #uuid "c811230b-9658-5987-9c1b-262af56e26cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2af483a4-08cb-52d4-89f8-45aa30404b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-11186" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/1840ce2886aa3546371998239757a5dc500449007dc511dc0860b1ce59a59d2c.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $x=\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}+2y,$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=2,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}+2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+2{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{2}+2\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{8}{3}-0)+2(2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "384ce5b6-40da-59a0-bc87-a1d726328cc2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Finite Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca032f50-1f40-54cf-9f46-d43796ab9f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291659} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2f0628-8c1c-530d-bdfd-8bb434e1c508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291660} {:answer/id #uuid "078800a8-cebe-58ce-9e17-9d758f699b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291661} {:answer/id #uuid "e34ea8be-25d3-5c8c-a9a0-ed09df3c7ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291662} {:answer/id #uuid "099d7abf-4a6c-53db-80d8-643b859ec24e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "078800a8-cebe-58ce-9e17-9d758f699b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{81}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291661} :answer-field/id #uuid "8572001a-818f-5e75-b818-8c8fa08fb31f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ef13261-2249-54c7-9c1e-84f17b27b72d" :question/math-academy-id "q-117485" :question/problem "Find the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=9y-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(9-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/bbcf218e7b6181c861ba4c804357cc53ae6004453914259a9fe98ad5f09ac4d4.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=9y-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=3.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(9y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{3}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-0)-(\\frac{(3)^{4}}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}-\\frac{81}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "963f66a5-f742-5b3b-8ae1-f0dbf268bff0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis by First Rewriting the Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9dd518e-7bce-5448-896b-5c2afe49512d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^2-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291669} {:answer/id #uuid "53ba15e0-d532-514c-ad3f-87da629b06d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9dd518e-7bce-5448-896b-5c2afe49512d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^2-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291669} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e63584e-aa9b-5412-afa2-f9536d28d011" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a144a16a-629d-56f3-8574-ea0e972aadca" :question/math-academy-id "q-316744" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}} ." :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ {e}^{y} & =3x \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,{e}^{y}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{2}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "963f66a5-f742-5b3b-8ae1-f0dbf268bff0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis by First Rewriting the Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6a9ae96-5722-51b5-9dea-72539452f983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291700} {:answer/id #uuid "e6fe71fb-5ffb-5f7c-97a7-bdeaee93de30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291701} {:answer/id #uuid "64142ebd-f4d5-5abd-b930-93e75013addf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291702} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e40b9f-c72a-568b-88c6-34d8387128f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291703} {:answer/id #uuid "111d8272-6085-500d-9322-5154ebcd762c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2e40b9f-c72a-568b-88c6-34d8387128f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291703} :answer-field/id #uuid "26b7bbc3-2473-559b-96e6-caa1992f1303" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de6ca82c-874b-5d47-8c60-3c70fc49188e" :question/math-academy-id "q-78896" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013903/assets/446dddc9ec5393005b5aa9fff9d45760c6f2e80da97fd459cdd74cf4f8575f71.png)\n\nFind the area bounded between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}+1,$ the $y$-axis and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x-1}+1 \\\\ y-1 & =\\sqrt[3]{x-1} \\\\ (y-1)^{3} & =x-1 \\\\ x & =(y-1)^{3}+1 \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}+3y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+3{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}-3\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}+3\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-0)-3(\\frac{1}{3}-0)+3(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-1+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "963f66a5-f742-5b3b-8ae1-f0dbf268bff0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area Bounded Between a Curve and the Y-Axis by First Rewriting the Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8da1ac9e-5d73-54a5-9acb-2d9b09fe55dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291714} {:answer/id #uuid "a47c8570-166e-5f0d-b4e9-da23b3447c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291715} {:answer/id #uuid "8051ada9-6c2b-524a-9b67-7fa1be1832f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291716} {:answer/id #uuid "997a9eff-fe9e-5679-a122-b7c98ef8efa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291717} {:answer/id #uuid "7e37ea78-ac29-5404-a478-c1274a3d8625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e37ea78-ac29-5404-a478-c1274a3d8625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291718} :answer-field/id #uuid "f99a5cb0-4488-5371-b233-09cb04d57303" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ac19116-a4f9-5daa-91e3-f0e9f4a15596" :question/math-academy-id "q-78900" :question/problem "Find the area between the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x) \\\\ {e}^{y} & =2-x \\\\ x & =2-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(2-{e}^{y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot y{|}_{-2}^{0}-{e}^{y}{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =2(0+2)-({e}^{0}-{e}^{-2}) \\\\  & =4-(1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
